      "text": "To solve for \\( x \\), we start with the given equation:\n\n\\[ x^2 = 729 \\]\n\nWe need to find the positive value of \\( x \\). Therefore, we take the square root of both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[ x = \\sqrt{729} \\]\n\nNow we need to determine the positive square root of 729. We can do this by finding the prime factorization of 729:\n\n\\[ 729 = 3 \\times 729 = 3 \\times (27 \\times 27) = 3 \\times (3 \\times 9 \\times 9) = 3 \\times (3 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 3) \\]\n\n\\[ 729 = 3^6 \\]\n\nTaking the square root of \\( 3^6 \\):\n\n\\[ \\sqrt{3^6} = 3^{6/2} = 3^3 = 27 \\]\n\nSince 729 is a perfect sixth power, \\( \\sqrt{729} \\) is indeed 27. Therefore, the value of \\( x \\) is:\n\n\\[ x = 27 \\]\n\nPutting the final answer in \\boxed{}:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{27} \\]",
